{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_5uroygoelzhwi4afoz7dmai34r3fzv356dkrz5ic57yta7ld7ufa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete — everything green, `c_src/` untouched.\n\n## What was verified\n\nThe library is a single C translation unit exporting one symbol: `void md5_digest(const tflac_md5 *m, tflac_u8 out[16])`.\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (all derived mechanically from the C, not assumptions):\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 1 exported C symbol; Rust `.so` exports it too; 0 missing, 0 non-libc undefined.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — a grep of every rejection mechanism proved the C has **no in-band error surface** (void return, 0 checks, 0 branches, 0 enums). So the 7 actionable rows (E1–E7) are pointer-contract faults, and 3 rows are documented N/A with a test pinning the header so the N/A can't silently rot.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 18 rows over 9 axes derived from the header + the actual emitted instructions (byte-lane mapping, values, field order, both operands' alignment, storage class, overlap, read/write exactness, reentrancy).\n\n**Phase B/C/D** — 26 differential tests (+3 unit), all driving **both** `.so` files through `libloading`; 29 tests × 2 profiles all pass, symbol diff empty, `cargo check`/`clippy` clean. `./verify.sh` automates the whole matrix and derives the feature power set from `Cargo.toml`.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed (Rust only)\n\nBoth were invisible to happy-path testing — every valid-input row passed before *and* after.\n\n1. **NULL pointers aborted (SIGABRT) instead of faulting (SIGSEGV).** `ptr::read_unaligned` carries a *library*-UB precondition assert, and rustc emits a codegen null-check for raw place derefs (`*p`) — both active under `debug-assertions`. The resulting panic crossing `extern \"C\"` aborts. Fixed by using primitives that carry only *language*-UB preconditions, plus `wrapping_add` instead of `add`.\n\n2. **The release optimizer narrowed the 4-byte field load, moving the fault boundary.** With only 1 readable byte at `m`, the C commits **0** output bytes (its full 4-byte load faults first) while release-Rust committed **1** byte. Fixed by making the 16 loads/16 stores volatile, which forbids narrowing, merging and reordering. Release Rust now compiles to the *same instruction sequence* as the C.\n\nBug 2 was only reachable because the error-path tests compare the **bytes committed before the fault** (via a `MAP_SHARED` buffer + fork-per-implementation), not merely \"both crashed\" — and only in the `release` profile, which is why I added the profile axis to compensate for this crate having no `[features]`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Only one feature combination exists (no `[features]` table, no `#ifdef`/`option()` in the C), so I added the `dev`/`release` profile axis as the substitute configuration dimension — that's what caught bug 2.\n- As an extra check, the C was also built at `-O2` into `$TMPDIR` (leaving `c_src/` untouched) and both suites re-run against it via a `HARVEST_C_SO` override: all 26 tests still pass, though the canonical `-O0` build remains ground truth.\n- The volatile accesses cost some optimizability, which is the deliberate trade for byte-exact fault/aliasing fidelity with the C."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":858,"cache_read_input_tokens":142624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":858,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7939aa8f-6ab7-4ee7-b46c-66a3183f6ea8","uuid":"4c2b8b49-5342-4bd4-b81c-0a936640e51f","timestamp":"2026-08-19T13:59:19.925Z"}
